  {
   "topic": "System",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Each wire is crimped into an SSH-003T-P0.2-H contact; the contact snaps into an SHR housing, held by a lance in the housing, and the housing plugs into a BM (top entry) or SM (side entry) header soldered to the board. The contact is a high-pressure design. The header also takes the SR series IDC socket."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Housing variants",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "Every SHR housing comes with protrusions (suffix -B) or without them (no suffix); the protrusions make the housing 2.0 mm wider (dimension B). The 16- and 20-circuit housings are made only with protrusions. The circuit-1 end is marked with a triangle."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Header variants",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Top-entry headers run from 2 to 16 circuits and side-entry headers from 2 to 15 and 20. Dimension A, circuit 1 to the last circuit, is (circuits - 1) × 1.0 mm; body width B is A + 3.0 mm. Each header has a tin-plated reinforcement tab at each end that is soldered to the board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Board layout",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Pads are seen from the side the connector mounts on. Signal pads are 0.6 mm wide on a 1.0 mm pitch; each end has a 1.2 mm wide reinforcement-tab pad whose inner edge sits 0.7 mm beyond the centre of the outermost circuit. Pad pitch is held to ±0.05 mm, accumulating to no more than ±0.1 mm along the row. The layout figures are reference values."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Part number: header",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "BM02B-SRSS-TB reads: BM = SMT top entry (SM = SMT side entry); 02 = circuits; B = assembled product; SR = series; S = natural (ivory); S = sub model number; TB = embossed tape packing."
